Subject: Retain names generated in function calls
Message-ID: <S76iCdDd9ps.ZbmjspbjvYwJ@manipulatr.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/manipulatr/c/S76iCdDd9ps/m/ZbmjspbjvYwJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hello,
I learned a while ago, from Hadley in one of his classes if I remember correctly, to avoid `sapply` in favor of `vapply` because of `sapply`'s possibility of returning a result of an unexpected type. In other words, it is safer to state explicitly what you want your values casted to using `vapply`.

I currently have a situation where I have an unnamed vector of values to which I want to apply a function. The nuance here that is mixing me up is that my function returns a named numeric. When I use `sapply`, the name is retained. When I use `vapply`, it is not. I turned to `plyr` for help hoping `laply` would retain the names, but it acts just like `vapply`. The only `plyr` solution I came up with was using a combination of `unlist` and `llply`, which I don't think is ideal. See code below for examples.

My instinct is that `laply` should retain the names. Or am I missing something here that I could do this all in one function call (but not using `sapply`)? Or should I just rework my code to avoid the structure/situation all-together?

Many thanks,
Doug

###
foo <- function(x){ names(x) <- paste0("foo.", x); return(x); }
values <- 1:3;

### Base R
sapply(values, foo);
# foo.1 foo.2 foo.3
# 1     2     3
vapply(values, foo, numeric(1));
# [1] 1 2 3

### plyr
library(plyr);
laply(values, foo);
# [1] 1 2 3
unlist(llply(values, foo));
# foo.1 foo.2 foo.3
# 1     2     3

From ua523f4070ecfcf39 at groups.google.com  Tue Mar 18 18:05:19 2014
From: ua523f4070ecfcf39 at groups.google.com (John Rauser)
Date: Tue, 18 Mar 2014 18:05:19 +0000
Subject: Adapting dplyr for more programmatic use?
Message-ID: <htt0kO9Dhds.I5_ilTlh-SgJ@manipulatr.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/manipulatr/c/htt0kO9Dhds/m/I5_ilTlh-SgJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hello all,

I have a large dataset that I'd like to summarize and plot several different ways.  I currently do this by importing the data into sqlite and then paste()ing together SQL queries inside a function.  I was very hopeful that I could replace the nasty code that assembles SQL queries with some very pleasant and readable dplyr code.  But it appears that dplyr in it's current form isn't really meant for this purpose.  Rather, it's primarily intended for interactive use.  To illustrate what I'm up to:

library(dplyr)
data<-tbl_df(data.frame(class1 = sample(letters, 50, replace=T),
    class2 = sample(letters, 50, replace=T),
    value = runif(50)))

# Both of these work
group_by(data, class1) %.% summarise(n=n())
group_by(data, "class1") %.% summarise(n=n())

# This does not
rollup<-function(data_tbl, column) {
    group_by(data, column) %.% summarise(n=n())
}
rollup(data, "class1")
rollup(data, "class2")

Of course, my real rollup() function is substantially more complex.

There was a thread not too long ago when Hadley suggested a workaround as below, and said that dealing with this issue was on his long-term roadmap for dplyr.

# This does work
column <- "class1"
eval(substitute(group_by(data, column),
    list(column=as.name <http://as.name>(column)))) %.% summarise(n=n())

My question is whether there isn't some simple 90% solution to my problem.  My R meta-programming skills are feeble, but I wonder whether I could write something vaguely akin to aes_string() from ggplot2 that would wrap group_by().  This 90% solution wouldn't have be to good enough to bake into the dplyr codebase, but could make the workaround above much more concise.

Is what I'm after possible?  Reasonable?  Any help would be appreciated.

Thanks!

-J

From ub756a7daa2c2aa6d at groups.google.com  Tue Mar 18 19:00:21 2014
From: ub756a7daa2c2aa6d at groups.google.com (Hadley Wickham)
Date: Tue, 18 Mar 2014 19:00:21 +0000
Subject: Re: Adapting dplyr for more programmatic use?
Message-ID: <htt0kO9Dhds.O5eVE2cfvsoJ@manipulatr.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <htt0kO9Dhds.I5_ilTlh-SgJ@manipulatr.groups.google.com>
References: <htt0kO9Dhds.I5_ilTlh-SgJ@manipulatr.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/manipulatr/c/htt0kO9Dhds/m/O5eVE2cfvsoJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi John,

For this specific case, dplyr does already provide a version of
group_by that doesn't do any special evaluation: regroup.  It's not
terribly well documented, but you need to pass it a list of symbols.
There's a few way to get that list depending on what your input is:

library(dplyr)

# As a bare variable
rollup <- function(tbl, column) {
  cols <- list(substitute(column))
  tbl %.% regroup(cols) %.% summarise(n = n())
}
mtcars %.% rollup(cyl)

# Multiple variables
rollup <- function(tbl, ...) {
  cols <- dots(...)
  tbl %.% regroup(cols) %.% summarise(n = n())
}
mtcars %.% rollup(vs, am)

# As a string:
rollup <- function(tbl, column) {
  cols <- list(as.name <http://as.name>(column))
  tbl %.% regroup(cols) %.% summarise(n = n())
}
mtcars %.% rollup("vs")

If you'd like to learn more metaprogramming, I'd recommend
http://adv-r.had.co.nz/Computing-on-the-language.html and
http://adv-r.had.co.nz/Expressions.html

Hadley

